    "excerpt": "Section/subsection name: The ColorPath Algorithm\nContent:\nWithout loss of generality, assume S[j1]is red. We now construct a more intricate neighbor- 277\nhood graph, G\u2032. UnlikeG, this graph is directed and weighted. For each pair of agents A\u2032278\nandA\u2032\u2032, there is an edge from A\u2032toA\u2032\u2032if the two agents possess adjacent beads. If any such 279\npair of beads has a red bead on the side of A\u2032, we call the edge good, and its weight is 0; 280\notherwise, we call it bad, and its weight is 1. 281\nFirst, we find a shortest path from A2toA1inG\u2032. Letk\u2032be the number of agents 282\nalong that path. Then, we move S[j1]to indexj2. Next, for each good edge, we move the 283\ncorresponding red bead across the adjacent cut. For each subpath of bad edges, we rerun the 284\noffline algorithm on the substring corresponding to the subpath. We will henceforth refer to 285\nthis procedure as ColorPath . 286\nNote that the number of edges in G\u2032is at most twice the number of cuts. Finding a 287\nshortest path takes O(k)time using Dial\u2019s algorithm [ 8], giving a running time of O(k+k\u2032m/k) 288\nforColorPath . Asym, this is the same as Path, but in practice, ColorPath will likely 289\nbe faster. 290\ncomplexity O(k+k\u2032m/k). 292"
